{:edb/db-before-t 2527 :edb/db-after-t 2528 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535840 50 #inst "2026-10-09T08:39:31.483Z" 13194139535840 true] [17592186128943 1100 "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_0^{1} \\int_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1 } 9\\sqrt{y^3+1}\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x}.$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186128943 1100 "Change the order of integration and evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_0^{1} \\int_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1} 9\\sqrt{y^3+1}\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139535840 false] [17592186128943 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/1045aaad8025b20309b92477b4a1cf98e138064210c6fbc960c64ec26c9951df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad \\sqrt{x} \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=0,\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt{x} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=y^2.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq y^2 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/40cbaaff270daddf4b60115cc272fbc8b165b83b8823d19de2c19802617ebdb4.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1}9\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}9\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{y}^{3}+1}[x\\,]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{y}^{2}({y}^{3}+1)^{1/2}\\,dy \\\\  & =3{[\\frac{2}{3}({y}^{3}+1)^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[2({y}^{3}+1)^{3/2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2(2)^{3/2}-2 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186128943 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1997/Source/Images/b061a691651d115b218622521988a608.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad \\sqrt{x} \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$, is\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$, is\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt{x} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=y^2\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as type II region. Its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq y^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1997/Source/Images/a2cd4b5f56b6c6f034e5205b72232a90.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{\\sqrt{x}}^{1}9\\sqrt{y^{3} + 1}dydx &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{y^{2}}9\\sqrt{y^{3} + 1}dxdy \\\\\n&= 9∫_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{y^{3} + 1}[∫_{0}^{y^{2}}dx]dy \\\\\n&= 9∫_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{y^{3} + 1}[x]_{0}^{y^{2}}dy \\\\\n&= 3∫_{0}^{1}3y^{2}(y^{3} + 1)^{1/2}dy \\\\\n&= 3[\\frac{2}{3}(y^{3} + 1)^{3/2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= [2(y^{3} + 1)^{3/2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 2(2)^{3/2} - 2 \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{2} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535840 false] [17592186128944 1100 "Change the order of integration in the double integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1\\int_{2x}^{3-x} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x .$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186128944 1100 "Change the order of integration in the double integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1\\int_{2x}^{3-x} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x$." 13194139535840 false] [17592186128944 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/47f6b583b2dd059ad8df49b0ca4374a964b4a28e91eb242a0dd3c7b8476fd26e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 2x \\leq y \\leq 3-x \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type II regions. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/35b4b57f26c7903189899ec284faa39bb70fb3a80397b772376358d4d86b3ec1.png)\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y),$  is \n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{y}{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the right boundary of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=3-y.\n$$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\Big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\Big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 2 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2x}^{3-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186128944 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1997/Source/Images/d5f8a21d03c9e61e36a15cd45faecbfa.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. This is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD=\\big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 2x \\leq y \\leq 3-x \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we need to split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1997/Source/Images/4e6985e72db38bb32ef0c05926b8ba59.png)\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y)$, is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{y}{2}\n$$\n\nAlso, the right boundary of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y)$, is\n$x=3-y$.\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\Big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{y}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\Big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 2 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 3-y \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{2x}^{3 - x}f(x, y)dydx &= ∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{y/2}f(x, y)dxdy + ∫_{2}^{3}∫_{0}^{3 - y}f(x, y)dxdy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535840 false] [17592186128945 1100 "Change the order of integration in the double integral\n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y$." 13194139535840 false] [17592186128945 1100 "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 \\int_{y^3}^{1} f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186128945 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/47b8a129674d4644593741607e0efd5dea1ffde45357f327e3af4e08d0bf423d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq1, \\quad y^3 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx = y^3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\sqrt[3]{x},\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny = 0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt[3]{x} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/e627a8750d3f5055b3936d47100fc51ae68926dfdc0c53d020638c9ef99f9b27.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{x}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186128945 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1997/Source/Images/25d522dde8ecc0a0a3957dae03108ac8.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq1, \\quad y^3 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y)$, is\n\n$$\nx = y^3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y = \\sqrt[3]{x}\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny = 0\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD= \\big\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq \\sqrt[3]{x} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1997/Source/Images/925312e310acb44953022570595a9942.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{y^{3}}^{1}f(x, y)dxdy &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{x}}f(x, y)dydx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535840 false] [17592186129124 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369507 13194139535840 true] [17592186129124 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369514 13194139535840 true] [17592186129124 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369521 13194139535840 true] [17592186129124 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369556 13194139535840 true] [17592186129124 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369563 13194139535840 true] [17592186129125 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369486 13194139535840 true] [17592186129125 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369493 13194139535840 true] [17592186129125 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369500 13194139535840 true] [17592186129125 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369549 13194139535840 true] [17592186129126 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369472 13194139535840 true] [17592186129126 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369479 13194139535840 true] [17592186129126 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369528 13194139535840 true] [17592186129126 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369535 13194139535840 true] [17592186129126 1020 #edb/ref 17592186369542 13194139535840 true] [17592186129613 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535840 true] [17592186129613 1100 "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186129613 1100 "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$∫_{0}^{2}∫_{0}^{x}f(x, y)dydx + ∫_{2}^{4}∫_{0}^{4 - x}f(x, y)dydx$" 13194139535840 false] [17592186129613 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/ea36f1ec1ddeb03dd8d133fb8d65b5d50b3fa07ba34495978d87f95742dee042.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type I regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le x}\\,\\cup \\,{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 4-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=4-x\\,⟹\\,x=4-y.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/e82098a4bb63f1d709a06b6a24da078594716d6a3cc0fa2e069af9dcd9154955.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369472 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369473 13194139535840 true] [17592186369472 1089 "q-132522" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369472 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535840 true] [17592186369472 1100 "Change the order of integration in the double integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369472 1113 #uuid "cda48084-fd07-57a7-b084-a3c04ecffe6a" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369472 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/a65b8ea83edc85a0b0ec3f3df6ee6f5bc841e9cb5d634aab963054f836965146.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+1\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y-1},\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=2.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 5,\\,\\sqrt{y-1}\\le x\\le 2},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/d10ba1db82f9d0366edb446f8bef564030fea2068066c7f05f388f0c9daa3414.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y-1}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369474 13194139535840 true] [17592186369473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369475 13194139535840 true] [17592186369473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369476 13194139535840 true] [17592186369473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369477 13194139535840 true] [17592186369473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369478 13194139535840 true] [17592186369473 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535840 true] [17592186369473 1041 "selection" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369473 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369474 13194139535840 true] [17592186369473 1110 #uuid "e5b23d66-ae49-511e-815d-97cf66e9d038" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369474 1034 "{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y-1}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369474 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535840 true] [17592186369474 1111 #uuid "c14626bd-0185-544a-bd6d-038a30e1fdfa" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369475 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y-1}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369475 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535840 true] [17592186369475 1111 #uuid "487185f9-2952-5dc0-a3b6-a9fc03088b6d" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369476 1034 "{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{y-1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535840 true] [17592186369476 1111 #uuid "3798647d-bb41-500b-b07e-815b27c09528" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369477 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y+1}}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369477 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535840 true] [17592186369477 1111 #uuid "e47fe0e5-72bb-5853-a857-9f84ec5c4eb3" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369478 1034 "{\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y+1}}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369478 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535840 true] [17592186369478 1111 #uuid "de26b309-5548-5387-94c8-7be223ba8459" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369479 1017 #edb/ref 17592186369480 13194139535840 true] [17592186369479 1089 "q-132526" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369479 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535840 true] [17592186369479 1100 "Change the order of integration in the double integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{{e}^{y}}^{5}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369479 1113 #uuid "ac4f939e-e716-5d27-b164-1d86e556f507" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369479 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/7e0a0b1860f316f7954e85b9d7f6567d9e0c05bf316cb8af3901037abe6fd17e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡5,\\,{e}^{y}\\le x\\le 5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y}\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 5,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/3c95dd90ad02ab708f5893e47e7d7d0c8a212c18aed1972fb1bba1362181c383.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\int }_{{e}^{y}}^{5}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369480 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369481 13194139535840 true] [17592186369480 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369482 13194139535840 true] [17592186369480