 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535840 true] [17592186369486 1100 "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1-e}^{0}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369486 1113 #uuid "b9a7843d-3020-5482-8aef-e872811d9165" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369486 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/3426e7bdd134bc9af5a3665f8c9c55a65514afa9dca5be112eabb37479c4c9ec.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type II regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,1-e\\le y\\le 0,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)\\le x\\le 1}\\,\\cup \\,{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le e-1,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\le x\\le 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)\\,⟹\\,y={e}^{x}-1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)\\,⟹\\,y=1-{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1-{e}^{x}\\le y\\le {e}^{x}-1},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/7d88d351b481859ec3e77350d3679387836d2e7f67325caca50e2f61cce9dcce.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1-e}^{0}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-y)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y+1)}^{1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-{e}^{x}}^{{e}^{x}-1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\