92186369528 1100 "Change the order of integration in the double integral ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2y-2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369528 1113 #uuid "55e02e74-398d-5605-8097-c13c491d8f3c" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369528 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/062d32beb0cab13297c21b2eab703fd5e598191c96daa93a9c27337af95a1930.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type II region, and its type II representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,2y-2\\le x\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx=2y-2\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{x+2}{2},\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=1.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le \\frac{x+2}{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071085/assets/543ffc57d0d32ae9a4bdefa87bd56deb7380061004b7737cf821117b4fb20e2f.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2y-2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{(x+2)/2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369530 13194139535840 true] [17592186369529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369531 13194139535840 true] [17592186369529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369532 13194139535840 true] [17592186369529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369533 13194139535840 true] [17592186369529 1000 #edb/ref 17592186369534 13194139535840 true] [17592186369529 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535840 true] [17592186369529 1041 "selection" 13194139535840 true] [17592186369529 1104 #edb/ref 17592186369531 13194139535